WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 52: The Great Hinckley Fire of 1894 and Heroic Engineer Jim Root

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:21.120
[Rebecca] On Saturday, September 1, 1894, an apocalyptic firestorm swept through the dry white pine forests of eastern Minnesota, incinerating the prosperous lumber town of Hinkley in less than two hours.

4
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:38.240
[Rebecca] In the midst of terrifying destruction, locomotive engineer Jim Root throttled engine 482 in reverse, backing his passenger train straight through a blazing wall of fire and across burning wooden bridges to carry 300 terrified souls to safety.

5
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:14.080
[George] The timber boom had stripped hundreds of millions of bored feet from the woods, leaving behind vast wasteland tracts covered in slashings, mountain high piles of resinous pine branches, brittle tops, and dry bark curring to kindling under the blazing summer sun.

6
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:21.360
[George] By late August, small smoldering brush fires dotted the railroad corridors between Duluth and the Twin Cities.

7
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.600
[George] Thick grey haze hung perpetually over Hinkley, a bustling lumber center of 1,200 residents.

8
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.640
[George] Street lamps were lit at noon, and locomotives operated with their cowcatcher headlamps burning through the choking smoke.

9
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:46.480
[George] The townspeople were accustomed to summer brush fires, viewing them as routine frontier nuisances, unaware that atmospheric conditions were aligning to create a firestorm of hurricane intensity.

10
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:58.160
[George] Lumber barons amassed immense personal fortunes cutting the ancient white pine stands, leaving behind an ocean of dry logging debris that transformed Pine County into an open air fire trap.

11
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:09.000
[George] The soil itself had baked dry beneath the blistering August sun, turning to powder beneath the hoofs of draft oxen hauling heavy loads of timber to the bustling riverside sawmills.

12
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:19.720
[George] Farmers in surrounding homesteads watched anxiously as the sun rose blood red through the permanent sulphur haze, remarking that the woods had never felt so ominous or brittle.

13
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:29.960
[George] On the afternoon of Saturday, September 1, two separate timber fires south of Hinkley merged under the influence of an aggressive cold front sweeping down from Canada.

14
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:39.160
[George] The colliding winds created an immense thermal updraft, forming a pyrocumulonimbus convection column that rose thousands of feet into the stratosphere.

15
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.400
[George] Air rushed inward at hurricane speeds to feed the thermal vortex.

16
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:48.280
[George] Temperature inside the column spiked past 1600 degrees Fahrenheit.

17
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:55.160
[George] Highly combustible pine gases vaporized and detonated in rolling fireballs that tumbled through the treetops.

18
00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:03.640
[George] Witnesses described hearing a sound like continuous rolling thunder or a hundred freight trains roaring simultaneously down a mountainside.

19
00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:14.920
[George] At two o'clock in the afternoon, total blackness descended upon Hinckley, punctuated only by the eerie scarlet glow of the forest crown fires advancing at 30 miles an hour.

20
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:25.240
[George] The atmospheric low-pressure system drew in blistering desert winds from the south, superheating the pine resins until the forest canopy began to smoke spontaneously.

21
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:34.680
[George] Flaming cinders the size of dinner plates rained down from the sky, igniting roofs and wooden sidewalks half a mile ahead of the main advancing firefront.

22
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:44.120
[George] Birds fell suffocated from the sky as convective winds sucked all moisture from the air, creating a roaring vacuum that pulled freight cars along the sidings.

23
00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:52.880
[George] As the firestorm devoured the outskirts of Hinkley, passenger train number 4, pulled by locomotive engine 482 of the St.

24
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.000
[George] Paul and Duluth Railroad, steamed northward toward the town on its regular run.

25
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] At the throttle was 43-year-old veteran engineer James Root.

26
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.360
[George] In the cab with him was fireman John McGowan.

27
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.240
[George] As the train approached the Hinkley depot, Root was met by an apocalyptic sight.

28
00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:20.320
[George] Hundreds of frantic men, women and children were fleeing the town in terror, running along the tracks toward the train, their clothing already smoking from falling embers.

29
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.120
[George] The depot itself was beginning to ignite.

30
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.920
[George] Root immediately slammed on the brakes, stopping the train on the main line.

31
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:38.960
[George] Passengers and crew threw open the vestibules and hauled frantic townspeople aboard, packing the coaches until human beings were jammed shoulder to shoulder in the aisles and on the car platforms.

32
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:50.320
[George] Engineer Jim Root checked the steam pressure gauges in engine 482, recognizing that the safety of hundreds of lives rested entirely on his mechanical skill and courage.

33
00:04:50.320 --> 00:05:01.600
[George] Station agent Edward Cowok stood at the telegraph key until the wires melted, sending desperate calls for relief trains to Duluth before helping women and children onto the waiting coaches.

34
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:11.280
[George] Root yelled instructions to McGowan over the deafening mechanical roar of the draft, checking the coal tender to ensure they had sufficient water for the reverse dash.

35
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:19.520
[George] With the tracks to the north completely engulfed in roaring flames, engineer Root realized there was only one possible avenue of escape.

36
00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:26.080
[George] Reverse the train southward toward Skunk Lake, an expansive, muddy marsh six miles down the track.

37
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:34.120
[George] Root threw the reversing lever, pulled the throttle wide, and locomotive 482 began backing into the darkness.

38
00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.560
[George] But the firestorm had already outflanked the rail corridor.

39
00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:42.920
[George] Within minutes, roaring walls of flame closed in on both sides of the moving train.

40
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:48.680
[George] The heat became so intense that the exterior paint blistered and peeled from the passenger coaches.

41
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:54.600
[George] Window glass cracked and exploded, showering terrified passengers with burning splinters.

42
00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:09.800
[George] Embers blew through the broken windows, setting luggage, coats and hair on fire as conductor Thomas Sullivan and his crew moved through the cars with water buckets, dousing small blazes while urging mothers to keep their crying children flat on the floor.

43
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:19.160
[George] Flying brands of burning birch bark slammed against the engine cab windows, showering the crew with sparks as the train backed through the roaring fire front.

44
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:31.320
[George] The coach roofs caught fire as the train rumbled across the smoking ties, and conductor Sullivan led bucket brigades of water from the tender to keep the passenger compartments from turning into cremation ovens.

45
00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:42.440
[George] The roar of the firestorm sounded like hundreds of artillery batteries firing simultaneously, shaking the locomotive frame as sparks and burning branches pelted the steel boiler.

46
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.960
[George] Conditions inside the locomotive cab were torturous.

47
00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:52.280
[George] A flying ember shattered the cab window, spraying shards of glass into Jim Root's neck and forehead.

48
00:06:52.280 --> 00:07:00.760
[George] The draft of superheated air sucked into the cab was so blistering that Root's trousers caught fire and his hands blistered against the steel throttle.

49
00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.760
[George] Overcome by carbon monoxide and intense heat, Root collapsed unconscious to the floorboards.

50
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:18.480
[George] Fireman McGowan, fighting his own agonizing burns, grabbed a bucket of water from the locomotive tender, doused Root's smoldering clothes, and hauled the engineer back up to his seat.

51
00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:25.920
[George] Root regained consciousness, blinked away the blood streaming into his eyes, and kept his hand firmly on the throttle lever.

52
00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.840
[George] Ahead in the smoke lay the wooden trestle over Grindstone Creek.

53
00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.560
[George] If the bridge had burned through, the entire train would plunge into the ravine.

54
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:44.080
[George] Root held his breath as engine 482 rumbled across the smoking timbers, which collapsed into the gorge just seconds after the train passed.

55
00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:53.840
[George] Root's hands were seared by the iron throttle lever, yet he refused to surrender control, knowing that stopping the train meant certain cremation in the woods.

56
00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:04.320
[George] McGowan kept shoveling coal into the roaring firebox despite his blistered hands, knowing that a loss of steam pressure on the slight uphill grade would doom every soul aboard.

57
00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:14.080
[George] McGowan soaked rags in the drinking water jug, pressing them against Root blistered neck while shouting encouragement into the deafening tempest of wind and flame.

58
00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:21.600
[George] Through sheer willpower and mechanical luck, locomotive 482 reached the marshy shallows of Skunk Lake.

59
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.040
[George] Root braked the train to a halt on the raised embankment.

60
00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:34.320
[George] Conductor Sullivan and the crew herded more than 300 passengers out of the scorching coaches and down into the muddy, 18-inch water of the swamp.

61
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:46.800
[George] Men, women, and children submerged themselves up to their chins in the foul, leech-filled mud, covering their heads with wet gunnasacks and coats to breathe the thin layer of air just above the water's surface.

62
00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] Wild deer, wolves, and black bears splashed into the mud beside them, all primal hostility forgotten in the shared instinct for survival.

63
00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:04.600
[George] Moments after the passengers evacuated, the train's wooden coaches burst into total flame on the embankment, lighting the swamp like a funeral pyre.

64
00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:15.560
[George] In the muddy shallows of Skunk Lake, terrified mothers held infants above the slimy water, praying aloud while wooden railroad cars burst into thirty-foot jets of flame.

65
00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:26.680
[George] When the swamp water began to boil along the margins, passengers splashed water over each other's faces, watching in awe as wild animals stood alongside them in peaceful silence.

66
00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:32.840
[George] By dawn the following morning, the fire had burned itself out against rain and natural clearings.

67
00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:44.360
[George] The devastation was absolute, over 400 square miles of timberland lay reduced to white ash, and 418 people had perished in Hinkley and surrounding settlements.

68
00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:54.840
[George] Yet, thanks to the courage of Jim Root, John McGowan, and the train crew, more than 300 souls survived Skunk Lake without a single passenger lost.

69
00:09:54.840 --> 00:10:04.200
[George] Root suffered severe degree burns and permanent lung damage, but he returned to the rails, heralded as an international hero of the industrial age.

70
00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:19.080
[George] The Hinkley disaster spurred Minnesota to establish its first state forestry agency and modern wildfire management laws, ensuring that the sacrifice and courage of that September afternoon would reshape conservation in the American North forever.

71
00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:29.320
[George] News of Jim Root heroic stand made international headlines, and the governor of Minnesota awarded him a gold medal for extraordinary valor in the line of duty.

72
00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:37.640
[Rebecca] Engineer Jim Root's reverse dash through the flames stands as one of the greatest feats of locomotive bravery in history.

73
00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:39.000
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

74
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:44.200
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we investigate an ancient stone unearthed in the Minnesota sod.

75
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.280
[Rebecca] The Kensington Runestone.

